# f2 = keeps
# Behind a guard of Dog the allocation names the prototype as that of a class does. That any guard of the class answers for what lies under `prototype` is a conclusion of the walk, which no test of the program's own shows.
# Where proofs are checked, the allocation keeps that form, and the function itself is asked: what it holds under `prototype` is the object of the class table.
# RUN(PLAIN): walk f2 mechanisms=all
# PLAIN: = NewObject -,
# RUN(CHECKED): walk f2 mechanisms=all testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime
# CHECKED: = NewObject -,
# CHECKED: = GetClassPrototype {{*}}[readsClassObjectItself]
# CHECKED: [[same]]: boolean @Boolean = StrictEq
# CHECKED-NEXT: CheckProof [[same]] checkedType=true {{*}}source=WhatIsRemembered
# RUN(QUIET): run-in-shell testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime globalThis.publish = () => { }; globalThis.change = () => { };
# QUIET: 30
# QUIET-NEXT: 1030
# QUIET-NEXT: exited with 0
# To store the same object again ends the guard of the name `prototype` and no other, and the conclusion stays true: a check that asked that guard would stop a program that is right.
# RUN(SAME): run-in-shell testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime globalThis.publish = () => { }; globalThis.change = (Dog) => { Dog.prototype = Dog.prototype; };
# SAME: 30
# SAME-NEXT: 1030
# SAME-NEXT: exited with 0
# RUN(REPLACED): run-in-shell testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime globalThis.publish = () => { }; globalThis.change = (Dog) => { Dog.prototype = { total() { return 100; } }; };
# REPLACED: 30
# REPLACED-NEXT: 1500
# REPLACED-NEXT: exited with 0
